{
  "analysis": "I can see 4 scripts. Permission issues are visible:\n- collect_data.sh has permissions --wx--x--x (no read permission for owner/group/other, only execute)\n- process_data.sh has rw-r--r-- (no execute permission)\n- run_pipeline.sh has rw-r--r-- (no execute permission)\n- generate_report.sh has rwxr-xr-x (fine)\n\nI need to read each script's content to understand the pipeline and find any logic issues. Let me examine all scripts.",
  "plan": "Read the content of collect_data.sh, process_data.sh, and generate_report.sh to find logic issues, dependency problems, or missing files. Then determine the fixes needed (permissions + any logic/dependency issues).",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat collect_data.sh\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat process_data.sh\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat generate_report.sh\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Now I understand all the issues:\n\n1. **run_pipeline.sh**: permissions `rw-r--r--` — no execute bit. Needs `chmod +x`.\n2. **collect_data.sh**: permissions `--wx--x--x` — has execute but NO read. A script can't be read by the kernel to execute without read permission. Needs `chmod +r` (or +rx).\n3. **process_data.sh**: permissions `rw-r--r--` — no execute bit. Needs `chmod +x`.\n4. **generate_report.sh**: has a broken shebang `#!/bin/nonexistent` — invalid interpreter. Needs to be fixed to `#!/bin/bash`.\n\nThe logic in each script looks fine. The main fixes are permissions and the shebang. Let me also verify `/data` is writable.",
  "plan": "1. Fix the shebang in generate_report.sh using sed.\n2. Set execute permissions: chmod +x on run_pipeline.sh, process_data.sh; chmod +rx on collect_data.sh (adds read + execute).\n3. Run the pipeline to verify it succeeds.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "sed -i '1s|#!/bin/nonexistent|#!/bin/bash|' generate_report.sh\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "chmod +x run_pipeline.sh process_data.sh\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "chmod +rx collect_data.sh\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "head -1 generate_report.sh; ls -la\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "All fixes applied:\n- generate_report.sh shebang corrected to #!/bin/bash\n- All four scripts now have execute permissions\n- collect_data.sh now has read+execute (rwxr-xr-x)\n\nNow I need to verify the pipeline actually runs successfully.",
  "plan": "Run ./run_pipeline.sh and check the exit/output. Then verify the output files were created in /data/output to confirm the full pipeline succeeded.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "./run_pipeline.sh\n",
      "duration": 1.0
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "echo \"EXIT: $?\"; ls -la /data/output/\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "collect_data.sh succeeded, but process_data.sh failed with 'cannot execute: required file not found'. This error typically occurs when the shebang's interpreter can't be found — often caused by a stray carriage return (CRLF) in the shebang line, making bash try to execute '/bin/bash\\r' which doesn't exist. I need to inspect process_data.sh's first line for hidden characters.",
  "plan": "Use cat -A to reveal hidden characters in the shebang of process_data.sh. If there's a carriage return, fix it. Then re-run the pipeline.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat -A process_data.sh | head -1\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "file process_data.sh collect_data.sh generate_report.sh run_pipeline.sh\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Found the real issue: process_data.sh has a CRLF line ending in its shebang (`#!/bin/bash^M$`). The `^M` (carriage return) makes the kernel try to run `/bin/bash\\r`, which doesn't exist. I should strip CR characters from all scripts to be safe.",
  "plan": "Remove carriage returns from all four scripts using sed. Then re-run the pipeline and verify all output files are created.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "sed -i 's/\\r$//' process_data.sh collect_data.sh generate_report.sh run_pipeline.sh\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat -A process_data.sh | head -1\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "./run_pipeline.sh\n",
      "duration": 1.0
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "echo \"EXIT: $?\"; ls -la /data/output/\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

